<!DOCTYPE html>
<!-- The page shell — one markup tree, two layouts. Wide: `#drawer` is the
     conversation column beside the chat, its header hidden. Narrow: `#drawer`
     is a full-screen overlay the `#topbar` menu button opens, and `#topbar` is
     what is hidden the other way round. Nothing here is per-device: the split
     is media queries in style.css, so resizing a window crosses it live.
     See design/decisions.md D_one_shell_two_layouts.

     Server-rendered snapshot of the transcript and status bar; public/js/app.js
     then connects an EventSource and applies deltas. Block divs are addressed
     by their transcript id — the SSE `block` event upserts by id, `remove`
     deletes by id, and the `snapshot` the connection opens with reconciles
     this whole list against the server's (D_subscribe_then_snapshot).

     `data-theme` carries the resolved `theme` cookie so the first paint is
     already in the right palette; the toggle's label names the theme it
     switches *to*, and app.js keeps both in sync after a click.

     `data-conversation` names what this tab is showing. It is also what the
     EventSource subscribes to, so the server sends this tab only its own
     conversation's frames — a turn running elsewhere never arrives here.

     `interactive-widget=resizes-content` is what keeps the composer above
     iOS's on-screen keyboard instead of behind it. -->
<!-- `data-thinking` is the resolved reasoning-disclosure default — "expanded"
     or "collapsed". The blocks below are already served in that state (a
     collapsed browser gets its `<details>` closed, Web::App#disclosed), so the
     first paint is right without a script; the attribute is what transcript.js
     resolves *streamed* blocks against, which arrive `<details open>` because
     the listener cannot know a per-browser preference. A per-block override
     lives on the block div; see public/js/transcript.js and design/decisions.md
     D_details_per_block. -->
<!-- `data-tools` is the resolved tool-line form — "short" or "verbose". Every
     tool block carries both forms and style.css shows the one this attribute
     names, so a flip is a single attribute write and no block re-renders;
     there is no JS behind it beyond that write. See design/decisions.md
     D_both_forms_in_the_block. -->
<html lang="en" data-theme="<%= theme %>" data-thinking="<%= thinking %>"
      data-tools="<%= tools %>" data-conversation="<%= conversation %>">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, interactive-widget=resizes-content">
  <title><%= title %> · pikuri-webui</title>

  <!-- Kuri, the mascot, at every size a browser asks for. Firefox takes the
       SVG; the 32px PNG is the fallback. iOS reads only `apple-touch-icon` for
       the home screen, ignores the manifest's icons there, and composites
       transparency onto black — which is why every PNG here carries an opaque
       plate rather than a bare mascot. See design/decisions.md D_icon_plate.

       `theme-color` is rendered per theme rather than left to the manifest, so
       iOS's status bar matches the palette the cookie actually resolved to;
       the manifest's own value can only ever be the dark default. -->
  <link rel="icon" href="/images/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
  <link rel="icon" href="/images/icon-32.png" sizes="32x32" type="image/png">
  <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/images/apple-touch-icon.png">
  <link rel="manifest" href="/manifest.webmanifest">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="<%= theme == 'light' ? '#fbfbfa' : '#1e1f22' %>">
  <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-title" content="pikuri">

  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/style.css">
</head>
<body>
  <!-- The conversation column, and the phone's drawer. The chrome sits *around*
       the nav rather than inside it: GET /conversations replaces the nav's
       innerHTML on every list change, and anything in there would need event
       delegation plus a theme-cookie read in that route. -->
  <div id="drawer">
    <header id="drawer-header">
      <button type="button" id="drawer-close" aria-label="Close the conversation list">&times;</button>
    </header>

    <nav id="sidebar" aria-label="Conversations"><%==
      erb(:_sidebar, layout: false, locals: { conversations: conversations })
    %></nav>

    <!-- Starting a conversation belongs with the list of conversations. It also
         has to live somewhere the phone can reach with the status bar stripped
         to one line of text — and duplicating these per layout would mean
         duplicate ids. -->
    <footer id="drawer-footer">
      <button type="button" id="new-conversation" class="flat-button"
              title="Start a fresh conversation (Ctrl+Shift+O)"
              aria-keyshortcuts="Control+Shift+O Meta+Shift+O">+ new conversation</button>
      <button type="button" id="theme-toggle" class="flat-button"
              title="Switch to the <%= theme == 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark' %> theme"
              aria-label="Switch to the <%= theme == 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark' %> theme"><%=
        theme == 'dark' ? '☀ light' : '☾ dark'
      %></button>
    </footer>
  </div>

  <div id="chat">
    <header id="topbar">
      <button type="button" id="drawer-open" aria-label="Show the conversation list"
              aria-expanded="false">&#9776;</button>
      <span id="topbar-title"><%= title %></span>
    </header>

    <main id="transcript">
      <% blocks.each do |block| %>
      <div class="block <%= block.kind %>" id="<%= block.id %>"><%== block.html %></div>
      <% end %>
    </main>

    <!-- Send and stop stay two buttons: sending mid-turn injects, so an arrow
         that becomes a stop square while busy would cost the injection
         (D_interloper_mid_loop_input). The stop button appears beside it.

         The composer precedes the status bar, as on the desktop it always has;
         the phone layout swaps them with `order` rather than a DOM reshuffle. -->
    <!-- This whole form is public/js/composer.js's: the textarea, the palette,
         the keyboard that drives both, and the send. That file names the ids
         below directly, so they are a contract between these two files —
         renaming one here means editing it there (D_feature_owns_its_file).
         There is no hidden `conversation` field because the composer is told
         which conversation it posts to at mount.

         The exception is #cancel, which sits here but is statusbar.js's: it
         is driven by the status frame, not by anything the composer knows. -->
    <form id="composer">
      <!-- The slash-command palette, server-rendered whole and filtered in
           place: the composer only toggles `hidden` per row. It anchors itself
           — absolutely positioned against the composer, so it floats above the
           textarea and tracks its auto-grow with no geometry in JS.

           Every name here needs an arm in app.js's `commands` map; see
           PikuriWebUI::Web::SlashCommand. -->
      <ul id="slash-menu" role="listbox" aria-label="Commands" hidden>
        <% commands.each do |command| %>
        <li id="slash-<%= command.name.delete_prefix('/') %>" role="option"
            aria-selected="false" data-command="<%= command.name %>">
          <span class="slash-name"><%= command.name %></span>
          <span class="slash-description"><%= command.description %></span>
        </li>
        <% end %>
      </ul>

      <!-- The message's images, and the way to add them, are
           public/js/attachments.js's: the tray of previews (a full-width row
           above the textarea, empty and hidden at rest), the + button and the
           file picker it opens. The caps are the server's own constants, so the
           browser holds a draft to exactly what POST /messages will take. The
           picker's types are the four it accepts; iOS converts a HEIC photo to
           JPEG for a list that does not name HEIC. -->
      <ul id="attachments" aria-label="Images to send" hidden
          data-max-images="<%= PikuriWebUI::Web::App::MAX_IMAGES %>"
          data-max-bytes="<%= PikuriWebUI::Web::App::MAX_IMAGE_BYTES %>"></ul>
      <input type="file" id="attach-input" accept="image/png,image/jpeg,image/gif,image/webp" multiple hidden>
      <button type="button" id="attach" aria-label="Attach images"
              title="Attach images — or paste or drop them here">+</button>

      <!-- The hint is a title, not the placeholder: a paragraph of placeholder
           wraps to three lines in a phone-width composer, and Chrome sizes an
           empty textarea to its wrapped placeholder, so the composer's
           auto-grow opens the box at three lines before a character is
           typed. -->
      <textarea name="prompt" id="prompt" rows="1" autofocus placeholder="Message…"
                role="combobox" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="slash-menu"
                title="Enter to send, Shift+Enter for newline; sending mid-turn injects; / for commands"></textarea>
      <button type="submit" id="send" aria-label="Send" title="Send">&#8593;</button>
      <button type="button" id="cancel" aria-label="Stop this turn" title="Stop this turn"
              <%= 'hidden' unless status[:busy] %>>&#9632;</button>
    </form>

    <!-- The model names the connection this conversation is on — reality, not
         the selection, so a switch that never landed is never advertised. The
         pending span holds a bare name and CSS draws the arrow, which is what
         lets /model read the intended name straight back out of it. -->
    <footer id="status-bar">
      <span id="status-state"><%= status[:state] %></span>
      <span id="status-pending"><%= status[:pending].positive? ? "#{status[:pending]} queued" : '' %></span>
      <!-- `data-missing` is the connection this conversation had saved before
           it left the config file. It rides the model span rather than a
           segment of its own because the /model dialog is the only place it is
           ever said, and this is what that dialog reads. -->
      <span id="status-model" data-missing="<%= status[:model_missing] %>"><%= status[:model] %></span>
      <span id="status-model-pending"><%= status[:model_pending] %></span>
      <!-- Two spans because they carry different weight: the ctx headline is
           the number worth reading, the per-turn Δ/↑/↓ tail beside it stays at
           the bar's own muted color. The wrapper is what the flex row
           right-aligns, so the pair travels as one. -->
      <span id="status-tokens">
        <span id="status-tokens-ctx"><%= status[:tokens] %></span>
        <span id="status-tokens-detail"><%= status[:tokens_detail] %></span>
      </span>
    </footer>
  </div>

  <!-- Dialog bodies, rendered here and cloned by app.js when a command opens
       one. Server-rendered for the same reason everything else is: markup the
       server can produce is never assembled in the browser. The chrome (title,
       ✕) belongs to dom.js's openDialog, so these carry only the body. -->
  <template id="tpl-trifecta"><%== trifecta %></template>

  <template id="tpl-theme">
    <ul class="picker">
      <!-- Which one is current is *not* rendered here: the theme changes in
           the page after this was served (this picker, or the drawer's
           toggle), so app.js marks it on open. -->
      <li><button type="button" class="picker-option" data-theme="dark">Dark</button></li>
      <li><button type="button" class="picker-option" data-theme="light">Light</button></li>
    </ul>
  </template>

  <!-- Rendered whole: the connections come from the config file, which cannot
       change without a restart. Which row is current is app.js's, for the same
       reason it is in the theme picker — the model moves in the page after
       this was served. The model id under each name is what tells two
       connections running one model on two servers apart; a connection with no
       `context_window` leaves pikuri's own probe to answer, so it prints
       nothing here. -->
  <template id="tpl-model">
    <p class="dialog-detail model-missing" hidden></p>
    <ul class="picker">
      <% models.each do |llm| %>
      <li><button type="button" class="picker-option" data-model="<%= llm[:name] %>">
        <span class="picker-name"><%= llm[:name] %></span>
        <span class="picker-detail"><%= llm[:model] %><%=
          " · #{llm[:context_window] / 1000}k" if llm[:context_window]
        %></span>
      </button></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  </template>

  <!-- Which row is current is app.js's, as in the theme picker: the default
       moves in the page after this was served. "Expanded" and "Collapsed"
       describe how a *new* reasoning block arrives; picking either also drops
       every per-block override, so the whole transcript follows
       (D_details_per_block). -->
  <template id="tpl-thinking">
    <ul class="picker">
      <li><button type="button" class="picker-option" data-thinking="expanded">Expanded</button></li>
      <li><button type="button" class="picker-option" data-thinking="collapsed">Collapsed</button></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="dialog-detail">Reasoning is always kept — this is only whether it
      starts open. Any block can be opened or closed on its own.</p>
  </template>

  <!-- Which row is current is app.js's, as in the pickers above. This one
       needs no transcript walk to go with it: both forms are already in every
       tool block, so the attribute this writes is the whole change. -->
  <template id="tpl-toollines">
    <ul class="picker">
      <li><button type="button" class="picker-option" data-tools="short">Short</button></li>
      <li><button type="button" class="picker-option" data-tools="verbose">Verbose</button></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="dialog-detail">Short names the tool and its first argument, then
      what came back. Verbose is the raw argument dump and the observation
      itself.</p>
  </template>

  <!-- One preview in the composer's tray; attachments.js fills in the img
       from the file in hand, which no server could have rendered. -->
  <template id="tpl-attachment">
    <li class="attachment"><img alt=""><button type="button" class="attachment-remove"
        aria-label="Remove image" title="Remove image">×</button></li>
  </template>

  <template id="tpl-unknown-command">
    <p><code class="unknown-name"></code> is not one of this shell's commands.</p>
    <p class="dialog-detail">Known commands: <%= commands.map(&:name).join(', ') %>.</p>
    <p class="dialog-detail">To send it as a message instead, give it company —
      anything with a space in it is never read as a command.</p>
  </template>

  <!-- A module, so app.js, composer.js and dom.js each get their own scope and
       no neighbour can quietly become a bag of globals. No build step is
       involved: the browser resolves the imports itself. -->
  <script type="module" src="/js/app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
